CM Trap dipole for 40 and 80 m: 35.1 m of 2 mm wire, 10 m above average ground, with a
CM parallel LC trap cut into each half 10.2 m from the centre.
CM Each trap is 8.2 uH across 60 pF, resonant at 7.18 MHz, with a coil Q of 200 - which
CM NEC sees as 74 kohms in parallel with the L and C (the LD 1 cards below). On 40 m the
CM traps are nearly open circuits, so the inner 20.4 m is the whole antenna: 87 + j2 ohms
CM at 7.1 MHz. On 80 m each trap is just its coil, and the 7.35 m beyond it is still in
CM circuit but loaded, so 35 m of wire resonates where 39 m of plain wire would:
CM 47 - j2 ohms at 3.65 MHz. Change FR to 3.65 to see it.
CM The traps are not free. At 7.1 MHz the efficiency reads 90.8 %: a tenth of the power is
CM heat in the two coils, because a trap on its own band sits at a current node with the
CM full end voltage across it. On 80 m, where it is only an inductor, they cost under 2 %.
CM The dimensions were tuned with this engine; a wire of a different thickness, height or
CM insulation lands a little differently, which is what the model is for.
CM EMWS example by ZR1JT. CC0 1.0 - public domain.
